{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\frac{7}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=\\frac{2}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{7}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{7}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{2}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92219bf9aff9fd31ee772f9ff06a5327b98253daf41eab7da8acf82a11f302b6"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1$\n- $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 0 & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92219bf9aff9fd31ee772f9ff06a5327b98253daf41eab7da8acf82a11f302b6"} {"question" "q-141091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-2{e}^{-2x}$\n- $y=-2{e}^{x}$\n- $y=2{e}^{-x}+1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-2{e}^{-2x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{-2x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(-2{e}^{-2x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-2{e}^{-2x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2{e}^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ -2{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\ -2{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4{e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 6{e}^{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2{e}^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2{e}^{-x}+1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ -2{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(2{e}^{-x}+1) \\\\ -2{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-4{e}^{-x}-2 \\\\ 2{e}^{-x} & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2{e}^{-x}+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92219bf9aff9fd31ee772f9ff06a5327b98253daf41eab7da8acf82a11f302b6"} {"question" "q-141096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,y(-2)=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{A}{x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-2)=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{x} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{(-2)} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92219bf9aff9fd31ee772f9ff06a5327b98253daf41eab7da8acf82a11f302b6"} {"question" "q-141112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-141112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-352663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3y}{x},\\,y(2)=4.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{x}^{3}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-352663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=A{x}^{3}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=4$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A(2)^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A(8) \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-352663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92219bf9aff9fd31ee772f9ff06a5327b98253daf41eab7da8acf82a11f302b6"} {"question" "q-352663" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"} {"question" "q-352663" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "235487ca1337bef8b7e5deca3670b715948765d9cccd0e662bfed3a51d164164"}] "usage" {"q-141096" []} "basis" 1042 "retractions" [[17592186101071 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} =-2y$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y =-2e^{-2x}$\n2. $y =-2e^{x}$\n3. $y = 2e^{-x} + 1$"] [17592186101071 :question/worked-solution "Check the derivative against $-2y$ for each candidate.\n\n- I: $y=-2e^{-2x}$ gives $y'=4e^{-2x}=-2y$, so it is a solution.\n- II: $y=-2e^x$ gives $y'=-2e^x$, whereas $-2y=4e^x$, so it fails.\n- III: $y=2e^{-x}+1$ gives $y'=-2e^{-x}$, whereas $-2y=-4e^{-x}-2$, so it fails.\n\nSelect **I only**."] [17592186101071 :question/difficulty 17592186045463]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098197 17592186098198 17592186098199 17592186098200 17592186098201] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098196 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098196 :answer-field/id] [17592186098196 :answer-field/key] [17592186098196 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
